// Per-machine Nextflow resource cap for this modest WSL2 dev box (~8.7GB RAM
// inside WSL2, no .wslconfig override -- see config/pipeline.yaml). Newer
// nf-core pipelines (confirmed 2026-09-20 against nf-core/rnaseq 3.26.0) have
// dropped the old `check_max()` Groovy-closure pattern entirely (0 uses in
// conf/base.config) in favour of Nextflow's native `process.resourceLimits`
// directive -- but only set that directive inside their own `test`/`test_gpu`
// profiles, not the general `docker` profile. That leaves the pipeline's own
// --max_memory/--max_cpus params with nothing left to feed (base.config's
// process labels like `process_low` now hard-code fixed memory, e.g. 12.GB,
// with no params.max_memory gate at all), so those flags silently do nothing
// on 3.26.0 even though they still work on older/other nf-core pipelines
// (e.g. nf-core/fetchngs 1.12.0) that still call check_max(). Symptom seen:
// FQ_LINT (process_low, 12.GB fixed) failed immediately with "Process
// requirement exceeds available memory -- req: 12 GB; avail: 8.7 GB" despite
// --max_memory 6.GB being set correctly in the resolved params.
//
// Fix: set resourceLimits directly via this config, included with `-c`
// alongside `-profile docker` for any nf-core pipeline invocation on this
// machine. This is the mechanism the nf-core template itself is moving
// pipelines onto, so keep using it (rather than re-adding --max_memory) as
// more pipelines migrate off check_max().
process {
    resourceLimits = [
        cpus: 4,
        memory: 6.GB,
        time: 240.h
    ]
}
